1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,087
In this lesson, you'll add contacts
to Kyntlo in two ways: one at a time,

2
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:07,035
and in bulk from a spreadsheet.

3
00:00:07,035 --> 00:00:12,250
You'll also see how Kyntlo stops the same
person from ending up on your list twice.

4
00:00:13,131 --> 00:00:17,849
Most contacts arrive on their own, from
your forms, calendars, chats and calls.

5
00:00:17,849 --> 00:00:22,269
But sometimes you'll want to add someone
by hand, like a walk-in customer.

6
00:00:22,269 --> 00:00:27,286
And when you first move to Kyntlo, you'll
want to bring your existing list with you.

7
00:00:28,136 --> 00:00:30,273
Let's start with a single contact.

8
00:00:30,273 --> 00:00:33,793
Click Add Contact, and a panel
slides in from the right.

9
00:00:33,793 --> 00:00:37,501
Fill in their first and last
name, and their email address.

10
00:00:38,327 --> 00:00:40,316
Then add their mobile number.

11
00:00:40,316 --> 00:00:43,952
Always include the country
code, like +44 for the UK,

12
00:00:43,952 --> 00:00:46,216
so your text messages reach them.

13
00:00:47,066 --> 00:00:48,811
Add a tag to describe them.

14
00:00:48,811 --> 00:00:50,426
Type it, and press Enter.

15
00:00:50,426 --> 00:00:55,725
Tags are labels you'll use to filter your
list, and to start automations later on.

16
00:00:56,574 --> 00:01:00,018
You can also choose who on
your team owns this contact,

17
00:01:00,018 --> 00:01:04,151
and switch on Do Not Disturb if
they've asked not to be contacted.

18
00:01:04,151 --> 00:01:05,153
Then click Save.

19
00:01:05,153 --> 00:01:07,408
Ruby is now at the top of your list.

20
00:01:08,258 --> 00:01:11,002
Kyntlo also watches for
duplicates as you type.

21
00:01:11,002 --> 00:01:15,322
Let's try adding someone with a phone
number that's already in the system.

22
00:01:16,173 --> 00:01:19,647
Kyntlo spots that this number
belongs to Emma Clarke,

23
00:01:19,647 --> 00:01:22,137
and offers to open her record instead.

24
00:01:22,137 --> 00:01:26,135
It recognises people by their
email address and phone number,

25
00:01:26,135 --> 00:01:29,675
which is why it's worth
collecting both on every form.

26
00:01:29,675 --> 00:01:33,017
One person, one record,
means one complete history.

27
00:01:33,867 --> 00:01:34,776
Now for bulk.

28
00:01:34,776 --> 00:01:38,345
First, export your old
contact list as a .csv file,

29
00:01:38,345 --> 00:01:41,354
from a spreadsheet or
your previous system.

30
00:01:41,354 --> 00:01:44,153
Then click Import, and drop the file in.

31
00:01:45,004 --> 00:01:49,250
Next, Kyntlo matches each column
in your file to a field in Kyntlo.

32
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:50,265
Check every row.

33
00:01:50,265 --> 00:01:54,321
Columns like treatment and last
visit can go into custom fields,

34
00:01:54,321 --> 00:01:57,046
and anything you don't
need can be skipped.

35
00:01:57,896 --> 00:01:59,592
Then choose how to import.

36
00:01:59,592 --> 00:02:02,593
Create and update is
usually the right choice.

37
00:02:02,593 --> 00:02:08,073
New people are added, and anyone you already
have is updated, instead of duplicated.

38
00:02:08,923 --> 00:02:13,378
Add a tag to every imported contact,
so you can find this batch later.

39
00:02:13,378 --> 00:02:16,496
And confirm these people
agreed to hear from you.

40
00:02:16,496 --> 00:02:19,551
Only import contacts who
have given you consent.

41
00:02:20,403 --> 00:02:21,533
Click Start import.

42
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:26,116
Kyntlo works through the file in the
background, so you can carry on working.

43
00:02:26,116 --> 00:02:28,913
You can follow its progress
under Bulk Actions.

44
00:02:29,762 --> 00:02:30,884
Here's a pro tip.

45
00:02:30,884 --> 00:02:34,913
Spend ten minutes tidying your
spreadsheet before you import.

46
00:02:34,913 --> 00:02:39,139
Remove blank rows, put first and
last names in separate columns,

47
00:02:39,139 --> 00:02:41,714
and add country codes to phone numbers.

48
00:02:41,714 --> 00:02:43,893
Clean data now saves hours later.

49
00:02:44,745 --> 00:02:46,082
Your contacts are in!

50
00:02:46,082 --> 00:02:50,285
Next, we'll open a contact record
and explore everything it holds,

51
00:02:50,285 --> 00:02:54,106
from the activity timeline to
notes, tasks and appointments.

52
00:02:54,106 --> 00:02:54,997
See you there.
